# Untitled

## Molecular Engineering of Metal–Organic Frameworks as Efficient Electrochemical Catalysts for Water Oxidation
Metal–organic framework (MOF) solids with their variable functionalities are relevant for energy conversion technologies. However, the development of electroactive and stable MOFs for electrocatalysis still faces challenges. Here, a molecularly engineered MOF system featuring a 2D coordination network based on mercaptan–metal links (e.g., nickel, as for Ni(DMBD)-MOF) is designed. The crystal structure is solved from microcrystals by a continuous-rotation electron diffraction (cRED) technique. Computational results indicate a metallic electronic structure of Ni(DMBD)-MOF due to the Ni–S coordination, highlighting the effective design of the thiol ligand for enhancing electroconductivity. Additionally, both experimental and theoretical studies indicate that (DMBD)-MOF offers advantages in the electrocatalytic oxygen evolution reaction (OER) over non-thiol (e.g., 1,4-benzene dicarboxylic acid) analog (BDC)-MOF, because it poses fewer energy barriers during the rate-limiting $\because0$ intermediate formation step. Iron-substituted NiFe(DMBD)-MOF achieves a current density of $100m\mathbf{A}c m^{-2}$ at a small overpotential of $280m\vee,$ , indicating a new MOF platform for efficient OER catalysis.
## 1. Introduction
The oxygen evolution reaction (OER) is crucial in generating promising carbonfree energy resources such as hydrogen, and electrocatalytic technology powered by renewable energy promises the efficient and continuous proceeding of OER.[1] As a result of the sluggish kinetics of the OER, the energy conversion efficiency of electrocatalytic water splitting devices is limited.[2] Today, ruthenium $(\mathrm{Ru})$ - and iridium (Ir)-based catalysts $(\mathrm{RuO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{IrO}_{2}$ ) are regarded as benchmark catalysts for OER due to their superior water oxidation activity.[3] $\mathtt{R u}$ and Ir are expensive and scarce, limiting their use in large-scale devices.[4] To date, the application of cost-effective and earth-abundant transition metal-based materials (e.g., metal oxides) has attracted great research interest.[5] It has been shown that transition metal oxides feature intrinsic electronic structures, allowing them to interact with oxygenated intermediates in water oxidation, which contributes to their OER catalytic abilities.[6] A metal d-band modulation that differs from structural engineering can be used to optimize the electronic structure of transition-metal-based catalysts.[7] The development of electrocatalysts for water oxidation depends on material design that allows greater tuning of structure characteristics.
Since 2016, metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), which consist of coordinated metal nodes and organic ligands, have emerged as a popular class of electrocatalysts for OER.[8] There is great promise in the construction of efficient water oxidation catalysts due to their accessibility to metal sites, tunable porosity and chemical functions, and well-defined periodic framework structures.[9] A well-defined MOF structure can also be used as a valuable model system for elucidating the structure-performance relationship of complex electrocatalysis systems.[9c,10] The MOF structure design by molecular engineering can readily adjust the coordination environment of MOFs by optimizing metal species and selecting organic ligands at an atomic level. MOF electrocatalysts are often constructed with carboxylic linkers such as terephthalate (or benzene-1,4- dicarboxylate, BDC),[8,11] 2,6-naphthalenedicarboxylate,[9a] and benzene-1,3,5-tricarboxylate.[12] Compared with the inorganic OER electrocatalysts, MOF catalysts offer more tailorable functionality around the metal-carboxylate units for inducing better proton-coupled electron transfer capabilities.[11a,13] Even though strategies have been devised to boost the OER activity of MOFs, such as ultrathin 2D nanosheets engineering,[8] lattice-strain tunability,[14] ligand-competition amorphization,[15] and metal hydroxide mimicking,[16] their low electroconductivity and poor chemical stability prevent them from being used as electrocatalysts in industrial applications.[17] There is still a need for new insights into the design of advanced MOF structures and associated molecular engineering strategies in water oxidation processes.
In recent years, nickel (Ni) components have been found to impart high activities to electrocatalytic oxygen evolution in alkaline media,[6b] as is the mixed-metal framework systems.[8,18] According to these studies, tuning metal components and modulating the electronic structures of MOFs can improve their electrocatalytic activities. Additionally, mercaptan groups $(\mathrm{-}\mathsf{S}\mathrm{H})$ can be added to the organic ligands in this manner. It is possible for the less electronegative sulfur (S) donor to offer stronger metal(d)– S(p) overlap than the oxygen (O) ligands, resulting in higher conductivity than nickel–oxygen (Ni–O) analogs.[19] As multidentate molecules (e.g., with 3 or more linking units) tend to build more robust MOF products than bidentate ones,[20] multiple thiol groups can be attached to a common linker like BDC.[21] As the soft thiol group tends to bond strongly with soft transition metal ions like Ni(II),[22] the nickel–sulfur (Ni–S) bonding in the MOF product will stand to enhance the stability as well the electrocatalytic performance.
In this work, such design materializes in a tunable Ni-MOF OER catalysts system based on the thiol-functionalized 2,6- dimercaptanbenzene-1,4-dicarboxylate (DMBD) linker. The individual 2D layer features both metal–sulfur (M–S) and metaloxygen (M–O) links, while the interlayer space contains a second type of metal ions bonded to the carboxyl O donors and bridged by water ligands. Instead of depositing Ni–S catalyst particles onto MOF matrices as in previous studies, this new MOF integrates Ni–S into the coordination links to functionalize MOF scaffolds.[23] The continuous rotation electron diffraction (cRED) technique is pivotal in solving the crystal structure from the microcrystals of the MOF sample, and the structure is verified by high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) analysis. Additionally, density function theory (DFT) analysis indicates a metallic electronic structure of Ni(DMBD)-MOF, suggesting enhanced electroconductivity from the integrated Ni–S function. Inspired by this novel electronic structure, we also examined its OER catalytic properties. With the incorporated Fe element, the bimetallic NiFe(DMBD)-MOF only requires a low overpotential of $280\mathrm{mV}$ reaching $100\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ . A water-splitting cell with NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF as anode exhibits a cell voltage of $1.50\mathrm{~V}@10\mathrm{~mA~cm^{-2}}$ in alkaline electrolyte with $\mathrm{Pt/C/NF}$ as cathode. This study not only opens a MOF platform for efficient and stable OER catalysis, but also showcases an effective strategy for leveraging molecular design and crystal engineering for achieving well-defined crystalline electrocatalysts.
## 2. Results and Discussion
As shown in Figure 1a, the thiol group with the neighboring carboxyl group in the DMBD molecule constitute a strong chelating motif for metal cations. Bader charge analysis was conducted to compare the charge distribution in the BDC and DMBD molecules. The charge is about $-1.29$ for each of the carboxyl O atoms of the BDC ligand (Figure 1a). With the thiol groups placed adjacent to the carboxyl groups as in DMBD, the charge slightly increases to $-1.32$ for the carboxyl O atom. The thiol groups here bring negligible influence on the hardness value of carboxyl O atoms in the DMBD molecule. Moreover, the charge on sulfur is calculated to be about $-0.82$ in DMBD, which is less negative than the O atom, and is consistent with the more covalent character of S as a coordinating site.[24] The difference of the Bader charges reflects the different chemical hardness values of oxygen and sulfur, and will largely underlie the different electronic structures and OER performances between the sulfur-containing DMBD MOFs and the oxygen-based BDC MOFs.
Crystalline DMBD MOFs were synthesized through solvothermal method between Ni cation precursors and DMBD molecule (Ni(DMBD)-MOF). BDC based Ni-MOF crystals were prepared as well according to the modified previous works via solvothermal reaction (Ni(BDC)-MOF).[25] The morphologies of Ni(BDC)-MOF and Ni(DMBD)-MOF were initially investigated by scanning electron microscopy (SEM). Ni(BDC)-MOF displayed stacked plate-like morphology with the length ranging from 7 to $12\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and an observed width of about $6~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ (Figure S1, Supporting Information). In contrast, Ni(DMBD)-MOF crystals exhibited a bamboo-leaves shaped morphology with a length of about $2\upmu\mathrm{m}$ and width of ${\approx}0.3~\upmu\mathrm{m}$ (Figure S2, Supporting Information). The differences of the morphologies and crystal sizes between the two networks can be ascribed to the incorporation of mercaptan groups in molecular structures for organic linkers, which brings additional nickel–thiolate connections. Analogy to chelating effect of multi-dentate ligands, higher connectivity of ligands can suppress the dissociation rate of ligands and enhance the association rate with metal nodes, thereby favoring the formation of small crystal.[20] Apparently, as revealed via distinguishable powder X-ray diffraction patterns (PXRD) in Figure S3, Supporting Information, the completely distinguished patterns were held in Ni(BDC) and Ni(DMBD) MOFs, suggesting the formation of a totally different crystal structure from that of Ni(BDC)-MOF.
The continuous rotation electron diffraction (cRED) technique was applied to determine the crystal structure for Ni(DMBD)- MOF (with phase purity of the bulk sample verified by the consistent XRD patterns between simulated and experimental ones; Figure 1g).[26] As displayed in Figures 1b–e, the reconstructed 3D reciprocal lattice derived from cRED datasets reveals a triclinic unit cell (space group $\mathrm{P}\overline{{1}}$ ) for Ni(DMBD)-MOF, with the parameters of $a=3.3\bar{0}00\bar{\dot{\mathrm{~A~}}}$ , $b=8.2400\textrm{\AA}$ , $c=9.790\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , $\alpha=73.83^{\circ}$ , $\beta$ $=81.80^{\circ}$ , and $\gamma=88.15^{\circ}$ (Table S1, Supporting Information). All non-hydrogen atoms were located directly from the electrostatic potential map by SHELXT and the results are exhibited in Figure 1f and Figure S4, Supporting Information. Ni(DMBD)-MOF presents two types of octahedral Ni sites: Ni-1 is bonded to four thiol S atoms equatorially and two carboxyl O atoms apically, resulting in a 2D coordination net; between the 2D nets is located Ni-2, apically bonded to two remaining O atoms of the carboxyl groups and four equatorial water molecules, which also bridge the octachora into a linear edge-sharing chain. All the characteristic peaks in the measured PXRD pattern for Ni(DMBD)-MOF can be indexed by this crystal phase determined by the cRED method (Figure 1g).
Additional transmission electron microscopy (TEM) studies further verify the cRED structure. As shown by the TEM image in Figure 2a, the Ni(DMBD)-MOF sample contains thin blades of nanocrystals ${\approx}2~{\upmu}\mathrm{m}$ long. As shown in Figures 2b,c, the high-resolution transmission electron microscopy (HRTEM) images reveal the side-view of the nanosheet. The distance of lattice fringes was evaluated to be about $9.50\mathrm{~\AA~}$ for Ni(DMBD)- MOF (Figure 2c), which is consistent with the $d$ -spacing of the (001) reflection of the crystal structure (Figure 1g). A top view HRTEM image of the Ni(DMBD)-MOF nanosheet is shown in Figure 2d. The corresponding fast Fourier transformation (FFT)
of Figure 2d along [101] and [101] zone axis in Figure 2e clearly disclosed the presence of (010), (101), and (111) planes, in good agreement with the simulated ED results along [101] zone axis (Figure 2f). As also shown in Figures 2g,h, the HRTEM and corresponding FFT images of Ni(DMBD)-MOF depict the atomic columns along the [101] and [101] zone axis, in which the yellow arrows specify the (010) twin boundary. The superposedsimulated SAED patterns along the [101] (red) and [101] (blue)
zone axis (Figure 2i) further validate the structural characterization.
X-ray absorption spectroscopy (XAS) and DFT calculations analyses were performed to explore the coordination environments and the electronic band structures, respectively. As shown in the Ni K-edge normalized X-ray absorption near-edge structure (XANES) spectra (Figure 3a), adding Ni–S sites yielded an average Ni valence state smaller than $+2$ , as revealed by the leftward shifted absorption edge position for Ni(DMBD)-MOF relative to that for Ni(BDC)-MOF. In order to further estimate the valence states of Ni atoms in Ni(BDC)-MOF and Ni(DMBD)- MOF, we performed the average edge energy $\left(E^{\mathrm{edge}}\right)$ by using the integral method as a function for oxidation state of Ni atoms in these materials (Figure S5, Supporting Information).[27] The average oxidation state of Ni components in Ni(BDC)-MOF and Ni(DMBD)-MOF was calculated to be $+2.32$ and $+1.94$ , respectively. This XANES result was also verified by the reduction of Ni species shown in XPS results (Figure S6, Supporting Information). The XANES and XPS results certified that incorporating Ni–S coordination associated with its electron-rich environment reduces the valence state of Ni, which may facilitate the surface reconstruction process of NiOOH species formation during OER and then promote the OER activity.[28] In the Fourier-transformed $k^{3}$ -weighted Ni K-edge extended X-ray absorption fine structure (EXAFS) spectroscopy, dominant peaks representing the nearest Ni–O or Ni–S coordination in MOF structure were observed (Figure 3b). No noticeable peaks that correspond to Ni–Ni bonds were observed in both Ni(BDC)-MOF and Ni(DMBD)-MOF profiles, indicating that MOF arrangement formed.[11a] The dominant peak at around $1.55\mathring{\mathrm{A}}$ can be ascribed to Ni–O bonds in the Ni(BDC)-MOF profile. Here we noted that the data mentioned were visible distances in the EXAFS spectra without phase correction. In comparison, the major peak in Ni(DMBD)-MOF profile shifted to around $1.65\mathrm{~\AA~}$ , corresponded to Ni-O/S coordination. The local atomic structure was further analyzed by the fitting results of Ni(DMBD)-MOF EXAFS profile, which showcased the coexistence of Ni-O bond with an average bond length of $2.04\mathrm{~\AA~}$ and Ni–S coordination with an average bond length of 2.29 Å (Figure S7 and Table S2, Supporting Information). The elongated average bonding between Ni atom and its coordinated atoms can be attributed to the incorporation of less electronegative S species and relatively weak bond energy of Ni–S. According to the XPS spectra of Ni(DMBD)-MOF, the existence of Ni–O and Ni–S bonds were further verified by comparing with fine-scanning $\mathtt{S2p}$ and O 1s XPS profiles of DMBD ligand (Figure S8, Supporting Information).
