To further enhance the OER activity, we introduced Fe species to prepare the mixed-metal systems, for example, NiFe(DMBD)- MOF, in which a small percentage of the Ni atoms are replaced by Fe. We achieved the metal substitution by directly reacting $\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}$ and $\mathrm{Fe}^{3+}$ cations with the DMBD or BDC linker in a onepot solvothermal process. The mass percentage of Fe was found to be $1.17\mathrm{\wt\%}$ in NiFe(DMBD)-MOF by inductively coupled plasma optical emission spectrometry (ICP-OES) (Table S4, Supporting Information). The SEM image in Figure S16, Supporting Information shows flower-like morphology with dense arrays of NiFe(DMBD)-MOF nanosheets covering uniformly and vertically on the NF scaffold. Atomic force microscopy (AFM) image shows the thickness of the NiFe(DMBD)-MOF nanosheets to be around $4.5\mathrm{nm}$ (Figure S17, Supporting Information). The electron energy loss spectroscopy (EELS) elemental mapping for NiFe(DMBD)-MOF of the selected area in Figure S18, Supporting Information exhibited the uniform distribution of C, O, Ni, Fe, S species, verifying the homogeneous nature of the bimetallic DMBD-based MOF products. As shown in Figure S19, Supporting Information, the simulated XRD pattern for NiFe(DMBD)- MOF was obtained by replacing some Ni sites with Fe atoms in the established model with a ratio of 14:1 (Ni:Fe), where no noticeable changes compared with that for Ni(DMBD)-MOF. The crystal structure for NiFe(DMBD)-MOF nanosheets was further verified by the simulated pattern (Figure S19, Supporting Information). Moreover, HRTEM images of NiFe(DMBD)-MOF reveal the lattice fringes distance to be ${\approx}0.95\mathrm{nm}$ , which was consistent with the $d$ -spacing values calculated from XRD pattern (Figure S20, Supporting Information). The above analysis indicated that the NiFe(DMBD)-MOF nanosheets were isostructural to the experimental Ni(DMBD)-MOF crystal structure. Similar results were observed from NiFe(BDC)-MOF (Figures S21–S24, Supporting Information).
According to the polarization curves in Figure 4d, the overpotentials of NiFe(DMBD)-MOF, NiFe(BDC)-MOF were found to be 263 and $350~\mathrm{mV},$ respectively, to deliver a current density of $50\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , which were much lower than that of the commercial counterpart $(\mathsf{R u O}_{2}/\mathsf{N F}$ , $\eta_{50}=450\mathrm{mV},$ ). The polarization curves for NiFe(DMBD)-MOF and NiFe(BDC)-MOF were also recorded after the consecutive CV scans (Figure S25, Supporting Information). The CV scanning curves after electrochemical activation of NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF and Ni(DMBD)-MOF/NF were measured, in which the redox potential of Ni active sites at 1.35 $\mathrm{\DeltaV_{RHE}}$ was observed to shift positively with Fe substituents in NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF (Figure S26, Supporting Information). Based on the previous research,[9c] we assume that the steadystate OER overpotentials could be reduced with positively shifted pre-OER Ni redox, in accordance with the reduced OER potentials in CV measurements (Figure S26, Supporting Information). In addition, the OER performance of NiFe(DMBD)-MOF (280 $\mathrm{mV}@100\ \mathrm{mA}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}.$ ) was also better than that of Ni(DMBD)- MOF $(420\mathrm{mV}@100\mathrm{mA}\mathrm{cm}^{-2})$ ) (Figure 4a), in accordance with the reported OER enhancement by Fe substitution in Ni-based catalysts. Particularly, only a small Tafel slope of $54.3~\mathrm{mV}$ dec−1 was observed in Figure $\mathtt{S27a}$ , Supporting Information, which is much smaller than the values of NiFe MOF/NF $(61.9\mathrm{mV}\mathrm{dec}^{-1})$ ), $\mathsf{R u O}_{2}/\mathsf{N F}$ $(72.4~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}})$ ), and bare Ni foam $(159.6~\mathrm{mV~dec^{-1}})$ ), confirming its rapid kinetics in OER. Faradaic efficiency (FE) for OER was measured to be $99.89\%$ , and $99.78\%$ on NiFe(DMBD)- MOF/NF, and NiFe(BDC)-MOF/NF systems, respectively, revealing their highly efficient energy conversion capability. Moreover, as shown in Figure S27b, Supporting Information, the excellent electrocatalytic OER activity of the NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF was also proved by its highest turnover frequency (TOF) and specific activity $(0.15~\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ and 0.51 mA $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\mathrm{catalyst}$ at an overpotential of $300~\mathrm{mV},$ respectively) (the ECSA and $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ of NiFe(DMBD)-MOF and NiFe(BDC)-MOF are shown in Figures S28 and S29, Supporting Information).
As shown in Figure S30a, after the durability test of $10\mathrm{\mA}\mathrm{\cm}^{-2}$ for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , the overpotential for NiFe(DMBD)- MOF/NF system to maintain the initial current density presented only $2.7\%$ increase. The OER polarization curves before and after this chronopotentiometry stayed virtually the same (Figure $\mathrm{\Delta}\mathrm{S}30\mathrm{b}$ , Supporting Information). In addition, the nanosheetlike morphology of NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF remained intact after this durability test (Figure S31, Supporting Information), demonstrating the stable capability of NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF for OER. However, compared with fresh NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF, the surfaces of the nanosheets got curved and rough after OER (Figure S31a–d, Supporting Information). The EELS elemental mapping for NiFe(DMBD)-MOF after stability test revealed that C, O, Ni, Fe, S species were uniformly distribute, which verified their homogeneous nature (Figure S31e, Supporting Information). After the OER stability test, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was employed to analyze the higher oxidation states of Ni and Fe sites (Figures S32 and S33, Supporting Information). The fitted area ratio correlated with divalent and trivalent oxidation states $(\mathrm{Ni^{2+}}/\mathrm{Ni^{3+}})$ in Figure S33b, Supporting Information was evaluated to be 2.50 in NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF after OER (compared with 2.61 in fresh NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF in Figure S33a, Supporting Information), indicating the mild oxidation of Ni elements. Oxidation was also found by evaluating fitted area ratio associated with divalent and trivalent oxidation states $(\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}/\mathrm{Fe}^{3+})$ for NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF after OER stability testing (0.39), in sharp contrast to that for as-prepared NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF (1.46) in Figure S33c,d, Supporting Information. After the long-term stability test for OER, the structure was characterized. The XRD pattern indicated that the crystal phase of NiFe(DMBD)-MOF vanished, suggesting its structure turned amorphous (Figure S34a, Supporting Information). Meanwhile, the signal of sulfur species in the XPS spectrum of NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF after stability test disappeared yet (Figure S34b, Supporting Information). It is likely that the crystalline order in NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF was disrupted by the formation of metal (oxy)hydroxides (M–OOH) under the high oxidation potential. In addition, this assumption was confirmed by the Raman analysis (Figure $\mathtt{S34c}$ , Supporting Information), in which new broad peak at about $550~\mathrm{cm^{-1}}$ appear, probably ascribed to Ni–O stretching vibration mode in nickel oxyhydroxide (NiOOH), suggesting the formation of NiOOH.[32] These results revealed the high electrocatalytic stability despite an irreversible conversion to amorphous structure taking place during OER. Meanwhile, the $C_{\mathrm{dl},}$ which is positive proportion to the ECSA, was studied after OER process. The value of $C_{\mathrm{dl}}$ is dramatically dropped to $1.6~\mathrm{mF~cm^{-2}}$ compared with that before OER $(14.3~\mathrm{mF~cm^{-2}}$ ). (Figures S35 and S36, Supporting Information). This result should be derived from the formation of the corrsponding M–OOH species after OER, coinciding with the loss of metal-sulfur configuration that can adjust the binding energy with oxygen-containing intermediate and thus may tune the $\mathrm{C_{dl}}$ result after OER activation.[33]
Furthermore, the durability of the NiFe(BDC)-MOF/NF and NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF electrodes at a higher constant current density of $250\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ were examined for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ for comparison (Figure S37, Supporting Information). After the $24\mathrm{h}$ duration, the anodic overpotential showed only a slight increase by $16.5\mathrm{mV}$ $(5.4\%$ activity loss). Unlike NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF catalyst, the overpotential of NiFe(BDC)-MOF/NF required to retain the current density of $250\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ over the duration test for $24\mathrm{h}$ manifested an elevation by $89.3~\mathrm{mV}$ $(22.4\%$ activity loss), which deteriorated significantly during the long-term stability test for OER. This difference demonstrates the effect of thiol linkers for enhancing the electrocatalytic stability toward OER, which can be due to the incorporation of Ni–S bonding in NiFe(DMBD)- MOF. Our catalyst underwent only a small anodic overpotential increase to maintain the current density of $10\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ over a long duration. At the higher constant current density to deliver $250\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ (for the chronopotentiometry analysis), very few catalysts have been tested. Nevertheless, our NiFe(DMBD)- MOF/NF electrode outperformed most of the current materials by presenting the least discrepancy of overpotential after a long-term OER testing, manifesting robust stability (Table S5, Supporting Information). Therefore, our work has indicated that the enhanced OER activity and stability could be induced by the incorporation of thiol-functionalized linker. Encouraged by OER activity and stability of NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF, we assembled a water-splitting full-cell in $1\ \mathrm{{w}\ K O H}$ with $\mathrm{Pt/C/NF}$ as the cathode and NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF as the anode, respectively. The polarization profile indicates that $\mathrm{Pt/C/NF||NiFe(DMBD){\cdot}M O F/N F}$ required the applied potential of 1.50 and $1.85\mathrm{~V~}$ to achieve the current densities of 10 and $100\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ , respectively, outperforming than that of $\mathrm{Pt/C/NF||RuO_{2}/N F}$ ( $1.54\mathrm{~V~}$ to achieve the current densities of $10\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , Figure 4e). The stability curves in Figure $4\mathrm{f}$ indicate that Pt/C/NF//NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF can consistently perform for more than $100\mathrm{h}$ at $100\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , highlighting the great potential for NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF to serve as anode materials for electrochemical water splitting.
## 3. Conclusion
As a result of the design and synthesis of a structurally welldefined MOF material featuring mercaptan-nickel coordination links, we have achieved a new crystal structure for Ni(DMBD)- MOF based on cRED datasets. HRTEM studies further confirmed the cRED structure. Computational results indicate a metallic electronic structure of Ni(DMBD)-MOF, suggesting enhanced electro-conductivity due to the Ni–S coordination links containing less electronegative sulfur donor. In accordance with the electronic structure of Ni(DMBD)-MOF, it obtained satisfying electrocatalytic activity. Experimental results and DFT calculations revealed a facilitated formation of $\mathrm{\DeltaO_{ads}}$ intermediates with decreased activation energy barriers in Ni(DMBD)-MOFs. A 2D NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF obtained after Fe species were introduced displays an overpotential of $280~\mathrm{mV}$ for a current density of $100\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ . The water-splitting electrolyzer with thiolfunctionalized NiFe(DMBD)-MOF/NF as anode electrode registers an applied cell-voltage of $1.50\mathrm{~V}@10\mathrm{~mA~cm^{-2}}$ in alkaline electrolyte assembled with $\mathrm{Pt/C}$ as cathode. This organic linkers engineering strategy not only affords a promising MOF structural platform for screening water splitting electrocatalysts, but shall motivate further pursuit of this molecular engineering strategy for accessing wider arrays of advanced MOF catalysts.
